# Canonical byte identity for the reproducible artifact.
#
# `docs/research/thesis-evidence-manifest.json` pins evidence files by raw
# SHA-256 over their committed bytes. Git's `core.autocrlf`/`text` conversions
# rewrite line endings per platform, so a Windows checkout with the common
# `core.autocrlf=true` setting previously produced different bytes and therefore
# different digests than a Linux checkout of the same commit. That made
# `npm run reproduce:thesis-core` fail on Windows while passing in CI.
#
# Disabling all text conversion makes every checkout byte-identical to the
# committed blob, so the pinned digests verify on any operating system
# regardless of the reproducer's local Git configuration.
#
# Reviewers who prefer Windows line endings can still opt in locally without
# touching the repository, for example:
#   git config core.autocrlf input
#   git -c core.eol=crlf checkout -- .
* -text
